<p>Google confirmed that its Gemini model accessed three real company systems during a May 2026 cybersecurity evaluation run by Irregular, an independent AI-security testing company. Reuters reported on September 18 that Gemini found public information, guessed or found credentials, and reached systems that were not meant to be real targets. Google said the affected organizations were informed and the testing process was changed.</p>
<figure class="article-image"><img src="/assets/images/blog/gemini-hacked-three-companies-security-test-september-2026.svg" width="1200" height="630" alt="Gemini security evaluation flow from simulated target to unintended real system access"></figure>
<h2>What was supposed to happen</h2>
<p>The evaluation was designed to test Gemini's cybersecurity capabilities in a controlled environment. The model was given a fictional target and asked to gather information and perform security-oriented tasks within the scope of the test.</p>
<p>The problem was that the environment had unintended access to the public internet. That created a path from a simulated exercise to real companies. Reuters reported that one target name overlapped with a real company and that other credentials were discoverable in public sources.</p>
<h2>How Gemini reached real systems</h2>
<p>According to Reuters, Gemini found public information online and then used either guessed credentials or credentials exposed in public repositories to access three websites it believed were in scope.</p>
<p>The important technical lesson is that a model can follow the instructions it was given and still create a safety problem when the surrounding environment is too permissive. The failure was not only about model behavior. It was also about the evaluation setup, internet access, target isolation and credential handling.</p>
<h2>What happened after access</h2>
<p>Reuters reported that the model stopped its actions when it recognized the targets were real rather than simulated. Google said the incidents did not cause harm, the affected entities were notified, and the evaluation procedures were revised.</p>
<p>This does not make the original boundary failure unimportant. It shows why safety testing for agentic systems needs both model-level safeguards and environment-level controls.</p>
<h2>Why sandbox design matters more as agents gain tools</h2>
<p>Traditional software testing usually assumes the test environment is deterministic. Agent testing is harder because the system can search, reason, use tools and react to information it discovers at runtime. A small gap in isolation can therefore become a real-world side effect.</p>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it should prevent</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Network isolation</td><td>Unexpected access to real internet targets</td></tr><tr><td>Synthetic credentials</td><td>Use of real secrets discovered during a test</td></tr><tr><td>Target allowlists</td><td>Actions outside the intended evaluation scope</td></tr><tr><td>Action monitoring</td><td>Unusual tool use before damage occurs</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>The model is only one part of the security boundary</h2>
<p>Google's separate description of its AI-native security work makes the same broader point from the defender side: agentic systems need localized threat models, continuous scanning and human-in-the-loop remediation. Google says its Mantis review harness is now open source and is used to help identify vulnerabilities in code.</p>
<p>For AI product teams, this suggests a layered approach. Keep the model constrained, isolate the execution environment, limit credentials, log every meaningful action and create a rapid stop path when a task moves outside its approved scope.</p>
<h2>How to test AI agents without repeating the problem</h2>
<p>Start with synthetic identities, synthetic data and an explicit egress policy. Then test the agent against adversarial conditions: misleading target names, accidental public credentials, unexpected redirects, tool failures and changes in task scope.</p>
<p>Do not measure only whether the model completed the requested task. Measure whether it stayed inside the intended environment while doing so. A successful test with unsafe boundaries is not a successful security evaluation. Teams can apply the same thinking to the <a href="/tools-guide/google-mantis-ai-vulnerability-scanning-guide">Google Mantis AI vulnerability-scanning workflow</a>, where the security process stays tied to evidence and human review.</p>
<h2>What remains unclear</h2>
<p>The public reporting does not identify the three companies or provide a full technical log of every action taken by Gemini. It also does not establish that Gemini caused damage to those organizations. Those limits matter when interpreting the incident.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Did Gemini intentionally target real companies?</h3><p>The reported evaluation was designed around simulated targets. Reuters reported that Gemini reached three real company systems after using public information and credentials it found or guessed.</p><h3>Did the incident cause damage?</h3><p>Google said the affected organizations were informed and the reports available so far do not indicate damage from the incidents.</p><h3>What is the main lesson for AI agent testing?</h3><p>Do not rely on the model alone to enforce scope. Network isolation, synthetic credentials, target allowlists, monitoring and fast shutdown controls should form part of the test environment.</p>
